      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1879, Fullerton became the temporary county seat of Nance County while the county government was still being organized.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://fullertonne.gov/history-of-fullerton",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Chautauqua era, special trains from Columbus brought crowds to Fullerton for a two‑week festival that even hosted the Barnum and Bailey Circus.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://fullertonne.gov/history-of-fullerton",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fullerton sits at the junction of U.S. Routes 14 and 22, a crossroads that helped shape its early development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fullerton%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area that became Fullerton was part of a Pawnee reservation in 1856 and was closed to settlement until the 1862 Homestead Act.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fullerton%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A historic church in Fullerton now houses a museum that chronicles the town’s early settlers, traders, and Mormon pioneers.",
